---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"
import { release113 } from "../../data/vector-releases"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="models">

          <p>
            Sign in to Vector to use free models, or connect a provider account or API key. The free models, their
            data policies and their limits are described below, followed by keys from environment variables, custom
            providers, models that are not in the catalog, and the lists that allow or block providers.
          </p>

          <h2 id="byok">Bring your own key</h2>
          <p>
            Connect a provider in Settings, or run <code>vector providers login</code> in the terminal, then
            select a model in the session composer. Model availability, context limits, pricing, and
            rate limits come from the provider. Vector's usage view tracks the information returned by
            those sessions.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            From 1.99.99, Vector no longer loads the former third-party hosted providers, even with an API key.
            Choose another provider before starting a task. Sign in with ChatGPT is off in Vector: connect OpenAI with an API key, or run your own installed Codex CLI from <a href="/docs/external-agents">Host locally</a> in the desktop app, where it signs in by itself. A ChatGPT sign-in saved earlier is no longer used. Native
            Copilot, xAI, GitLab OAuth, Poe and DigitalOcean sign-in flows are paused pending Vector's own registrations;
            API-key methods remain where supported.
            Claude Code, Codex, Cursor CLI and the other agents in Host locally still use your own installed commands
            and their own sign-in.
          </p>

          <h2 id="free-models">Free models inside of Vector</h2>
          <p>
            Sign in to Vector and the Free models inside of Vector are there at no cost to you: they are provided by
            Vector, on Vector's own OpenRouter account, through Vector's server, which checks your account and its
            limits. You don't need an OpenRouter account of your own. Every model is listed at a zero price
            with tool-capable endpoints, and a request never switches to a paid model or provider. The section appears
            while Vector's service has free models switched on and has models to serve.
          </p>
          <p>
            From desktop 1.99.111 and the next CLI release, Vector offers every free model OpenRouter lists that can
            use tools, which Vector's agent works through. Google's Lyria previews and NVIDIA's content-safety
            classifier are left out because they list no tools, and a model whose free endpoints have been down all
            day returns once they answer again. A new free model appears on the next daily refresh. NVIDIA's free
            Nemotron models are included; NVIDIA's terms for them allow trial and evaluation use. Each model carries
            the data policy Vector recorded for the provider serving it, reviewed on 17 October 2026, and one Vector
            has not reviewed yet is treated as one that may train:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>No training:</strong> Cohere's North Mini Code (Cohere, kept up to 30 days), Dots3-Note
              Preview (AtlasCloud, which does not publish how long it keeps prompts) and XingChen's Xing4.0 (Phala).
              Each request asks OpenRouter to deny data collection. Google's Gemma 4 26B and 31B (Google AI Studio,
              kept up to 55 days) are also provided by Vector, with a small “US only” tag in model lists. This is a
              label only; Vector does not check your location.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>May train on your prompts:</strong> Poolside's Laguna S 2.1 and XS 2.1 (free until 31 October
              2026), Liquid's LFM2.5-2.6B, Thinking Machines' Inkling and Inkling Small, NVIDIA's Nemotron models,
              OpenRouter's Free Models Router, and every free model Vector has not yet reviewed, such as Recursive's
              Flint. Their providers may log your prompts, code and outputs and use them to train their models. The
              Free Models Router sends each request to a free model OpenRouter picks at random.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The model picker, the terminal's model list and <code>vector models</code> show
            “May train on your prompts” on those models. Before your first prompt to one, Vector asks you once,
            and nothing is sent until you accept. That one acceptance covers every model with the label, including
            ones Vector has not reviewed yet, until you withdraw it. A prompt to one of them falls back only to
            another such model from the same provider, never to a different provider. An invited guest in a shared
            workspace is not offered these models, since only the workspace owner can accept the notice. Once a guest
            has worked in a session, nothing from that session goes to them, your own prompts in it included, because
            the guest's messages are part of what each request sends. In the terminal,
            <code>vector models --accept-training</code> accepts
            and <code>vector models --decline-training</code> withdraws; in the desktop app, Withdraw is on the
            Free models inside of Vector card in the Help panel. Vector itself never trains on your prompts.
            Vector never picks one of these models for you ahead of a no-training model. Desktop 1.99.108 and
            CLI 1.99.99 are offered no-training models only.
          </p>
          <p>
            Free models provided by Vector have a per-minute and a daily limit for each account, and OpenRouter's own
            limits for Vector's account also apply. A coding task can use many requests, since each tool step is one.
            At a daily limit, Vector says when more free tokens are available, and at a per-minute limit when to try again. To keep working before then, choose a model
            from another provider you've connected, or add your own provider key in Settings → Providers.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prompts are processed by OpenRouter and the selected model provider. Vector does not request zero data
            retention for these models: most of their providers keep prompts and outputs under their own policies,
            and OpenRouter records Phala, which serves Xing4.0, as keeping none. The policies rely on the providers'
            published terms and OpenRouter's records, not an independent audit. They do not cover Vector's local
            history, OpenRouter's account logging or other connected services. Review them before sending
            confidential code. Other provider connections have their own terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            OpenRouter is also an ordinary provider in Settings → Providers. If you connect an OpenRouter account of
            your own there, its free models are listed under OpenRouter with that account's other models, each named
            with “(free)” after it, such as “Qwen3 Coder (free)”, and run on that account's own limits and privacy
            settings. The composer's model button shows the same name once one is chosen. Free models inside of Vector
            lists only the models provided by Vector, which keep running on Vector's account while yours is connected.
            Each row's tooltip says where it runs: Provided by Vector, or Your OpenRouter account. A key stored only in
            a repository's <code>provider.openrouter.options.apiKey</code> does not list them. From
            1.99.99 to 1.99.108, connecting OpenRouter was the way to start free; from desktop 1.99.109 it is signing
            in to Vector.
            <a href="https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/provider-selection">OpenRouter routing documentation</a>
            describes the data-collection and provider controls.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-models">Models in the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector models</code> lists the supported model catalog. Connect a provider with <code>vector providers login</code> to use its models. Pass a provider id to filter and <code>--verbose</code> for metadata such as per-token cost. The catalog is bundled with the release, and from 1.99.99 Vector refreshes it from Vector's mirror, <code>https://vectordev.ai/models</code>, once it has loaded and then every hour, keeping the last catalog it had when the mirror cannot be reached. <code>VECTOR_MODELS_URL</code> points it at another mirror, <code>VECTOR_MODELS_PATH</code> selects a local catalog file and stops the refresh, and <code>VECTOR_DISABLE_MODELS_FETCH=1</code> stops the refresh too. <code>--refresh</code> fetches at once, but only when <code>VECTOR_MODELS_URL</code> is set. With <a href="/docs/local">Vector Local</a> on, the refresh is blocked unless you allow it or the mirror is on this computer.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector models
vector models anthropic --verbose</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            Signed in with <code>vector login</code>, the free models provided by Vector are listed under
            <strong>Free models inside of Vector</strong>, marked Provided by Vector. Display names omit
            <code>:free</code>; selectors retain it. Human guidance, including the “May train on your prompts” label
            from the next CLI release, goes to stderr, while stdout keeps plain model selectors.
          </p>
          <p>
            Models that expose reasoning effort take <code>--variant</code>. The level resolves from the flag, then your saved preference, then what the session last used, and your choice is kept per provider and model.
          </p>
          <p>
            {release113.since} when you leave effort on Default, the request's size sets it, and a learned default may drop Thorough's extra effort (Settings → Memory → Workflow insights). See <a href="/docs/tokenomics#learned-defaults">Learned defaults</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="environment-variables">Keys from environment variables</h2>
          <p>
            A provider in Vector's catalog is connected when one of its environment variables is set, such as
            <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code> or <code>GROQ_API_KEY</code>. The catalog,
            <code>https://vectordev.ai/models/api.json</code>, lists each provider's variables under <code>env</code>.
            When a provider has one variable, its value is the key; a provider that takes several values, such as
            Amazon Bedrock or Azure, is connected when any of them is set. GitHub Copilot is not connected from the
            environment while its sign-in is paused.
          </p>
          <p>
            In the terminal, <code>vector providers list</code> shows the keys saved with
            <code>vector providers login</code> and then, under Environment, each variable it found. The desktop app
            reads the environment of your login shell when it starts on macOS and Linux, so a key exported in your
            shell profile works there too; on Windows it uses the environment Vector was started with. Restart Vector
            after changing a variable. Settings → Providers &amp; keys tags such a provider
            <strong>Environment</strong> and offers no Disconnect for it, since the key is not Vector's to remove:
            unset the variable instead.
          </p>

          <h2 id="custom-providers">Custom providers</h2>
          <p>
            Any server that speaks the OpenAI-compatible API can be a provider: a company gateway, a proxy, or a model
            server you run. In the desktop app, open Settings → Providers &amp; keys and choose
            <strong>Connect</strong> on <strong>Custom provider</strong>.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Server</strong><span>Ollama, LM Studio, vLLM, llama.cpp and SGLang fill in their usual address on this computer, and Other leaves the address to you. <strong>Find servers</strong> looks for those servers on the computer Vector's engine runs on, and <strong>Identify</strong> reads the server at the address you typed, sending the API key and headers you entered. Either fills in the models the server reports, with their context size and whether they call tools and read images, and leaves out Ollama's cloud models.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Provider ID and Display name</strong><span>The ID names the provider in settings and in model selectors, such as <code>gateway/coder-large</code>. It takes lowercase letters, numbers, hyphens and underscores, and cannot be one Vector already has. The display name is what model lists show.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Base URL</strong><span>Where requests go, starting with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code> and usually ending in <code>/v1</code>. A plain <code>http://</code> address that is not on this computer is marked Not encrypted.</span></li>
            <li><strong>API key</strong><span>Optional. Vector keeps it with its other provider keys, not in a settings file. Type <code>{"{env:NAME}"}</code> instead to have Vector read the key from the environment variable <code>NAME</code>.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Requests at once</strong><span>Shown once you pick a server. Left empty, a server on this computer or your network takes one request at a time, and any other server has no limit.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Models</strong><span>Each model's ID as the server names it, a display name, its context and reply limit in tokens, and whether it calls tools and reads images. With a context and no reply limit, the reply limit is a quarter of the context, at most 8,192 tokens.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Headers</strong><span>Optional headers sent with every request, for a gateway that authenticates another way.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Submit</strong> saves the provider in Vector's own settings on the computer Vector's engine runs
            on, as <code>provider.&lt;id&gt;</code> using the <code>@ai-sdk/openai-compatible</code> package, so it is
            available in every repository. A server that Find servers or Identify read is also added to your
            <a href="/docs/local#servers">Vector Local</a> model servers, after Vector asks you when that would loosen
            Vector Local; if you decline, its models stay off while Vector Local is on. <strong>Disconnect</strong>
            removes the provider's key and adds its ID to <code>disabled_providers</code>.
          </p>
          <h3 id="provider-config">Providers in vector.json</h3>
          <p>
            The same provider can be written in <code>vector.json</code> yourself (see
            <a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration files</a> for where it goes), which also lets you use any AI
            SDK package rather than the OpenAI-compatible one:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "provider": {
    "gateway": {
      "name": "Company gateway",
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "env": ["GATEWAY_API_KEY"],
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://llm.example.com/v1",
        "headers": { "X-Team": "platform" },
        "timeout": 600000
      },
      "models": {
        "coder-large": {
          "name": "Coder Large",
          "limit": { "context": 131072, "output": 8192 },
          "tool_call": true
        }
      }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>npm</dt><dd>The AI SDK package that talks to the server, <code>@ai-sdk/openai-compatible</code> when a provider Vector does not know names none. Packages Vector ships with load at once; any other is installed into Vector's cache the first time one of its models is used, and a <code>file://</code> URL loads a module from your disk. Vector calls the package's export whose name starts with <code>create</code>, with the provider's options.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>name</dt><dd>The name shown in settings and model lists.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>env</dt><dd>The environment variable that holds the key.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.apiKey</dt><dd>A key in the file itself. Write <code>{"{env:NAME}"}</code> here rather than the key.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.baseURL</dt><dd>Where requests go. In a repository's <code>vector.json</code> it must be <code>https://</code>, or <code>http://</code> to this computer (<code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code> or <code>[::1]</code>); Vector leaves out a repository's provider with any other address.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.headers</dt><dd>Headers sent with every request. A model's own <code>headers</code> are added to them.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.timeout</dt><dd>How long one whole request may take, in milliseconds, or <code>false</code> for no limit.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.chunkTimeout</dt><dd>Stop a streamed reply when no new chunk arrives within this many milliseconds.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options.concurrency</dt><dd>How many requests Vector sends the server at once, as Requests at once does.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>models</dt><dd>The provider's models, described below.</dd></div>
          </dl>

          <h2 id="custom-models">Add a model that isn't in the catalog</h2>
          <p>
            Under <code>provider.&lt;id&gt;.models</code>, a key that is not already one of that provider's models adds
            one, for a provider in the catalog as well as a custom one, and you select it as
            <code>&lt;provider&gt;/&lt;key&gt;</code>. A key that names a catalog model changes only the fields you
            give. This adds a fine-tuned model to OpenAI under a shorter name:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "provider": {
    "openai": {
      "models": {
        "support-tuned": {
          "id": "ft:gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14:acme::A1b2C3d4",
          "name": "Support (fine-tuned)",
          "limit": { "context": 1047576, "output": 32768 },
          "cost": { "input": 0.8, "output": 3.2 },
          "attachment": true,
          "modalities": { "input": ["text", "image"] }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <dl class="doc-rows">
            <div><dt>id</dt><dd>The model's name in requests, when it differs from the key.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>name</dt><dd>The name shown in model lists.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>limit</dt><dd><code>context</code> and <code>output</code> in tokens, and optionally <code>input</code>. Vector compacts a session before the context fills; with no context it never compacts that model's sessions on its own, and Vector's agent refuses a model with no context on a server on this computer or your network.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>cost</dt><dd><code>input</code>, <code>output</code>, <code>cache_read</code> and <code>cache_write</code> in US dollars per million tokens, and <code>context_over_200k</code> with the same fields for a long-context price. A model Vector does not know has no price without it.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>tool_call</dt><dd>Whether the model calls tools; true unless you say otherwise.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>reasoning</dt><dd>Whether the model reasons, which gives it effort levels.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>attachment and modalities</dt><dd>Whether it takes attachments, and <code>modalities.input</code>, the inputs it reads: <code>text</code>, <code>image</code>, <code>audio</code>, <code>video</code> and <code>pdf</code>.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>temperature</dt><dd>Whether Vector may send it a temperature; off for a model Vector does not know unless you turn it on.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>options and headers</dt><dd>Provider options and headers sent with every request to this model.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>variants</dt><dd>The model's effort levels, described next.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            <code>variants</code> are the effort levels <code>--variant</code> chooses by name and the desktop's effort
            slider moves across. Each is a set of provider options sent with the request, in the form the model's
            package takes them. A model with <code>reasoning</code> on already has the levels Vector knows for its
            package, such as low, medium and high, set with <code>reasoningEffort</code>, for many models on
            <code>@ai-sdk/openai-compatible</code>. A model whose ID contains <code>qwen</code>, <code>kimi</code>,
            <code>glm</code>, <code>minimax</code>, <code>grok</code>, <code>deepseek-r1</code>,
            <code>deepseek-v3</code>, <code>deepseek-chat</code> or <code>deepseek-reasoner</code>, common on
            self-hosted servers, gets none or only a fixed pair, so write its <code>variants</code> yourself. Your
            entries add levels or change them, and
            <code>"disabled": true</code> removes one. Here a server that accepts a higher setting gets a
            <code>max</code> level, and <code>low</code> goes:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "provider": {
    "gateway": {
      "models": {
        "coder-large": {
          "reasoning": true,
          "variants": {
            "max": { "reasoningEffort": "max" },
            "low": { "disabled": true }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

          <h2 id="provider-lists">Allow and block providers</h2>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>enabled_providers</strong><span>When set, only the providers it lists load. Every other provider is ignored, with its keys, environment variables and settings. The free models provided by Vector are the provider <code>vector</code>.</span></li>
            <li><strong>disabled_providers</strong><span>The providers it lists never load, even with a key. A provider in both lists stays off.</span></li>
            <li><strong>whitelist and blacklist</strong><span>Under <code>provider.&lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>whitelist</code> keeps only the models it lists and <code>blacklist</code> removes the ones it lists, by model key.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "enabled_providers": ["anthropic", "gateway"],
  "provider": {
    "anthropic": { "whitelist": ["claude-opus-5", "claude-sonnet-5"] }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            A repository you have not trusted can only narrow these lists. To keep a model out of the desktop's model
            picker without removing it, switch it off in Settings → Default models instead.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
